---
/**
 * Python Runner tool page.
 *
 * `client:load` like the other tool pages: the island is the page. The
 * interpreter itself is still lazy, so this only hydrates the editor.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import PyRunner from '../../components/tools/PyRunner';
---

<ToolLayout slug="python-runner">
  <PyRunner client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Most of the work on this site is a Python package with no interface. A README can describe
      what one does and a code block can show how it is called, but neither answers the question
      someone actually has, which is what happens when you run it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The usual answer is a notebook service or a hosted sandbox. Both mean an account, a cold start
      measured in tens of seconds, and a container somewhere executing a stranger&rsquo;s code at
      somebody&rsquo;s expense. For showing what a small package does, that is a great deal of
      machinery.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      There is already a computer in front of the reader. <a
        href="https://pyodide.org"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Pyodide</a
      > is CPython compiled to WebAssembly, and it runs in the tab: the interpreter is real, the standard
      library is complete, and <code>micropip</code> installs wheels from PyPI at runtime. Nothing is
      executed on a server, so there is no account, no queue, and no way for one visitor&rsquo;s code
      to affect another&rsquo;s.
    </p>
    <p>
      It runs in a Web Worker, which is the part that matters for a page anyone can type into. A
      runaway loop is not interruptible: Pyodide is synchronous WebAssembly, so
      <code>while True: pass</code> on the main thread would freeze the whole page including the stop
      button. In a worker it can be terminated, which is what the Stop button and the sixty second ceiling
      both do. The tab stays responsive either way.
    </p>
    <p>
      The environment picker is a registry rather than a hardcoded list, so the same component
      mounts on a project page with just that project&rsquo;s package selected. The
      <code>particle-wave</code> environment installs the published wheel from PyPI and runs the real
      four-stage extraction pipeline, which takes about two and a half seconds on a synthetic image.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No network from inside Python.</strong> A browser tab cannot open a socket, so
        <code>requests</code>, <code>urllib</code> and anything built on them fail. Cross-origin rules
        apply to whatever is left.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A compiled extension needs a WebAssembly wheel that somebody published.</strong>
        Nothing can be compiled in a tab. Pyodide prebuilds around 340 packages including NumPy, SciPy
        and pandas; outside that set, a package with a C or Rust extension only installs if its maintainer
        ships a wasm wheel, and most do not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The <code>railtracks</code> environment does not install, and says why.</strong> It needs
        pydantic 2.11 or newer, this interpreter has 2.10.6, and the newer
        <code>pydantic-core</code> publishes WebAssembly wheels only for CPython 3.14 where Pyodide is
        on 3.13. Past that, LiteLLM pulls in <code>tokenizers</code> and <code>fastuuid</code>, both
        Rust extensions with no wasm build at any version. All three were reproduced rather than
        assumed, and all three resolve themselves the day those wheels appear.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Roughly two to five times slower than native.</strong> Fine for a demonstration and useless
        for a benchmark. The <code>timeit</code> sample makes the gap visible rather than hiding it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stop loses everything.</strong> Terminating the worker discards the interpreter and every
        installed package with it, because that is the only way to interrupt running Python. The next
        run starts cold.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
